1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,986
A twenty-seven-billion-parameter AI model can now come in

2
00:00:02,986 --> 00:00:05,835
a file barely larger than six gigabytes.

3
00:00:05,835 --> 00:00:08,226
And a nine-billion-parameter model can take almost

4
00:00:08,226 --> 00:00:10,185
the same amount of space. So if

5
00:00:10,185 --> 00:00:12,193
your graphics card has eight gigabytes of

6
00:00:12,193 --> 00:00:14,260
video memory, which one should you download?

7
00:00:14,582 --> 00:00:16,818
The obvious answer is the bigger model.

8
00:00:16,818 --> 00:00:20,340
More parameters, more intelligence, same memory. Except

9
00:00:20,340 --> 00:00:22,263
that answer skips the part that determines

10
00:00:22,263 --> 00:00:24,498
whether your local AI setup is genuinely

11
00:00:24,498 --> 00:00:27,160
useful, or just technically capable of launching.

12
00:00:27,480 --> 00:00:29,507
Because a model file fitting on your drive

13
00:00:29,507 --> 00:00:31,490
is not the same as a model running

14
00:00:31,490 --> 00:00:34,146
comfortably on your GPU. And a model producing

15
00:00:34,146 --> 00:00:36,310
one impressive answer is not the same

16
00:00:36,310 --> 00:00:38,910
as handling your actual workload all afternoon.

17
00:00:39,230 --> 00:00:41,622
Today we're putting four common memory budgets under

18
00:00:41,622 --> 00:00:46,057
a microscope: eight, twelve, sixteen, and twenty-four gigabytes

19
00:00:46,057 --> 00:00:49,170
of VRAM. We'll use real published model files,

20
00:00:49,170 --> 00:00:51,153
check where their memory goes, and build

21
00:00:51,153 --> 00:00:53,260
a practical decision rule for each class.

22
00:00:53,580 --> 00:00:56,937
No imaginary token-per-second scores. No assumption that a

23
00:00:56,937 --> 00:01:01,192
compressed model automatically performs like its full-precision parent.

24
00:01:01,192 --> 00:01:03,344
And no pretending that every GPU with the

25
00:01:03,344 --> 00:01:05,970
same memory capacity behaves the same way.

26
00:01:06,294 --> 00:01:09,143
Start with the mistake underneath almost every local

27
00:01:09,143 --> 00:01:12,231
AI hardware guide. People compare model size to

28
00:01:12,231 --> 00:01:15,501
VRAM and stop there. An eight-gigabyte card,

29
00:01:15,501 --> 00:01:18,960
a six-gigabyte download, two gigabytes left. Simple.

30
00:01:19,280 --> 00:01:21,680
But those six gigabytes are primarily the model's

31
00:01:21,680 --> 00:01:25,166
stored weights. During inference, the runtime also needs

32
00:01:25,166 --> 00:01:28,639
working memory, conversation state, and other buffers.

33
00:01:28,639 --> 00:01:30,382
A vision model may need a separate

34
00:01:30,382 --> 00:01:33,363
visual encoder. The operating system and whatever

35
00:01:33,363 --> 00:01:35,322
else is running on your GPU may

36
00:01:35,322 --> 00:01:37,270
already be using some of that memory.

37
00:01:37,592 --> 00:01:39,863
The difference matters because running out of headroom

38
00:01:39,863 --> 00:01:43,289
changes the experience. Depending on your software, loading

39
00:01:43,289 --> 00:01:46,089
may fail, some layers may move to ordinary

40
00:01:46,089 --> 00:01:48,890
system RAM, or performance may become much worse

41
00:01:48,890 --> 00:01:51,378
than you expected. That's why our question isn't,

42
00:01:51,378 --> 00:01:53,277
can the file fit? It's, can the

43
00:01:53,277 --> 00:01:55,210
whole workload fit with room to operate?

44
00:01:55,530 --> 00:01:58,478
Now look at an actual eight-gigabyte option. Unsloth

45
00:01:58,478 --> 00:02:02,865
publishes a four-bit Qwen three-point-five, nine-billion-parameter model

46
00:02:02,865 --> 00:02:06,014
in GGUF format. The Q-four-K-M file is

47
00:02:06,014 --> 00:02:09,115
about five-point-six-eight gigabytes. That is a real

48
00:02:09,115 --> 00:02:11,747
published file size, not a benchmark, and

49
00:02:11,747 --> 00:02:14,330
not a guarantee about total GPU allocation.

50
00:02:14,650 --> 00:02:16,683
On an eight-gigabyte card, it gives you a

51
00:02:16,683 --> 00:02:19,436
sensible starting point for local text work. You

52
00:02:19,436 --> 00:02:23,162
can experiment with summarizing notes, explaining code, drafting

53
00:02:23,162 --> 00:02:25,980
emails, or asking questions about a short document.

54
00:02:26,300 --> 00:02:28,470
But don't jump straight to the model's maximum

55
00:02:28,470 --> 00:02:31,799
advertised context, and don't assume image understanding is

56
00:02:31,799 --> 00:02:33,938
free. If your setup needs an additional

57
00:02:33,938 --> 00:02:36,670
vision projector, that consumes more memory too.

58
00:02:36,990 --> 00:02:39,445
And here is the counterintuitive part. The Qwen

59
00:02:39,445 --> 00:02:44,205
three-point-eight, twenty-seven-billion-parameter model also has extreme low-bit community

60
00:02:44,205 --> 00:02:47,727
quantizations. One published one-bit-class file is about

61
00:02:47,727 --> 00:02:51,669
six-point-one-nine gigabytes. On paper, that is extraordinarily

62
00:02:51,669 --> 00:02:54,460
close to the nine-billion model's four-bit download.

63
00:02:54,780 --> 00:02:57,732
But these are not equivalent representations. Much more

64
00:02:57,732 --> 00:03:00,653
aggressive compression can change the model's accuracy and

65
00:03:00,653 --> 00:03:03,862
reliability, and some formats need specific runtime

66
00:03:03,862 --> 00:03:06,194
support. You cannot conclude that the tiny

67
00:03:06,194 --> 00:03:09,126
twenty-seven-billion file is the better assistant just

68
00:03:09,126 --> 00:03:11,870
because its original model had more parameters.

69
00:03:12,190 --> 00:03:14,106
If I had eight gigabytes and needed a

70
00:03:14,106 --> 00:03:16,726
dependable everyday tool, I would begin with a

71
00:03:16,726 --> 00:03:20,283
reasonably quantized smaller model, not the most spectacular

72
00:03:20,283 --> 00:03:22,690
parameter count I could squeeze into a download.

73
00:03:23,011 --> 00:03:25,571
I'd only move to the extreme compression after

74
00:03:25,571 --> 00:03:27,530
checking real answers on the tasks I care

75
00:03:27,530 --> 00:03:30,342
about. That is a recommendation based on memory

76
00:03:30,342 --> 00:03:32,758
and quality trade-offs, not a claim that we've

77
00:03:32,758 --> 00:03:34,880
benchmarked those two versions head to head.

78
00:03:35,200 --> 00:03:38,320
Twelve gigabytes changes the calculation. It sounds

79
00:03:38,320 --> 00:03:40,267
like only four more gigabytes, but

80
00:03:40,267 --> 00:03:41,880
it opens a useful middle ground.

81
00:03:42,200 --> 00:03:46,530
Unsloth's Qwen three, fourteen-billion-parameter Q-four-K-M file is

82
00:03:46,530 --> 00:03:49,258
about nine gigabytes. That leaves more room

83
00:03:49,258 --> 00:03:50,989
than trying to force a much larger

84
00:03:50,989 --> 00:03:54,006
model into a tiny quantization, although context

85
00:03:54,006 --> 00:03:56,250
and runtime overhead still have to fit.

86
00:03:56,586 --> 00:03:58,377
For someone working with code, this is an

87
00:03:58,377 --> 00:04:00,685
interesting class of hardware. You can ask a

88
00:04:00,685 --> 00:04:03,173
model to explain a function, suggest a small

89
00:04:03,173 --> 00:04:06,440
patch, review a test, or transform structured text.

90
00:04:06,760 --> 00:04:09,544
But distinguish answering a coding question from running

91
00:04:09,544 --> 00:04:12,453
an autonomous coding agent. An agent may repeatedly

92
00:04:12,453 --> 00:04:15,926
read files, call tools, receive outputs, and build

93
00:04:15,926 --> 00:04:18,342
a long history of the work. That history

94
00:04:18,342 --> 00:04:21,203
can make context memory and prompt-processing time

95
00:04:21,203 --> 00:04:23,290
as important as the weight file itself.

96
00:04:23,619 --> 00:04:24,989
Here's the decision I would make at

97
00:04:24,989 --> 00:04:27,129
twelve gigabytes. If I wanted better

98
00:04:27,129 --> 00:04:29,617
reasoning on short, contained tasks, I

99
00:04:29,617 --> 00:04:31,790
would try a fourteen-billion four-bit model.

100
00:04:32,110 --> 00:04:35,010
If I needed longer sessions, several documents, or

101
00:04:35,010 --> 00:04:37,341
greater responsiveness, I would compare it with a

102
00:04:37,341 --> 00:04:40,731
smaller nine-billion model using more headroom. The bigger

103
00:04:40,731 --> 00:04:43,060
one is an option, not an automatic winner.

104
00:04:43,380 --> 00:04:46,654
At sixteen gigabytes, the temptation gets stronger.

105
00:04:46,654 --> 00:04:49,707
You can download some twenty-seven-billion-parameter quantizations

106
00:04:49,707 --> 00:04:51,840
that are smaller than sixteen gigabytes.

107
00:04:52,183 --> 00:04:55,477
For example, Unsloth lists a dynamic three-bit Qwen

108
00:04:55,477 --> 00:05:00,537
three-point-eight, twenty-seven-billion file at around thirteen-point-one gigabytes.

109
00:05:00,537 --> 00:05:04,410
Another four-bit-class variant is around fourteen-point-three gigabytes.

110
00:05:04,730 --> 00:05:07,690
Both numbers are real download sizes. Neither proves

111
00:05:07,690 --> 00:05:11,055
a clean full-GPU experience at your preferred context

112
00:05:11,055 --> 00:05:13,928
length. Thirteen-point-one gigabytes of weights on a

113
00:05:13,928 --> 00:05:17,053
sixteen-gigabyte card leaves less than three gigabytes

114
00:05:17,053 --> 00:05:21,200
before runtime overhead. Fourteen-point-three leaves even less.

115
00:05:21,520 --> 00:05:24,550
If you enable vision, increase context, run another

116
00:05:24,550 --> 00:05:27,339
GPU application, or encounter a backend that

117
00:05:27,339 --> 00:05:30,150
needs larger buffers, the margin can disappear.

118
00:05:30,470 --> 00:05:32,968
This is where people confuse a successful launch

119
00:05:32,968 --> 00:05:35,179
with a good purchase decision. Imagine you

120
00:05:35,179 --> 00:05:37,595
finally load the larger model. It answers

121
00:05:37,595 --> 00:05:39,790
a short question. You post the screenshot.

122
00:05:40,110 --> 00:05:42,959
Then you paste a big repository file, expand

123
00:05:42,959 --> 00:05:45,603
the context, and discover that generation slows

124
00:05:45,603 --> 00:05:48,091
down or the backend starts offloading. The

125
00:05:48,091 --> 00:05:50,351
screenshot was real. The conclusion that your

126
00:05:50,351 --> 00:05:52,810
card comfortably runs that workload was not.

127
00:05:53,130 --> 00:05:55,659
So sixteen gigabytes gives you two reasonable

128
00:05:55,659 --> 00:05:58,243
routes. A fourteen-billion model at a less

129
00:05:58,243 --> 00:06:01,020
aggressive quantization can be the steady choice,

130
00:06:01,020 --> 00:06:03,340
with more space for context and overhead.

131
00:06:03,660 --> 00:06:06,031
A twenty-seven-billion model at three bits can be

132
00:06:06,031 --> 00:06:08,940
the experiment, if you accept the quality trade-off

133
00:06:08,940 --> 00:06:11,464
and carefully check memory use. The right answer

134
00:06:11,464 --> 00:06:14,817
depends on your job. Short reasoning, coding, document

135
00:06:14,817 --> 00:06:17,858
analysis, and long multi-step agent runs do not

136
00:06:17,858 --> 00:06:20,290
stress a machine in exactly the same way.

137
00:06:20,610 --> 00:06:22,739
Now we get to twenty-four gigabytes, where the

138
00:06:22,739 --> 00:06:26,333
twenty-seven-billion class starts to look much more practical.

139
00:06:26,333 --> 00:06:30,287
Unsloth lists a Qwen three-point-eight, twenty-seven-billion dynamic

140
00:06:30,287 --> 00:06:34,169
Q-four-K-M file at about sixteen-point-five gigabytes, and

141
00:06:34,169 --> 00:06:37,580
a dynamic Q-five-K-M file at about nineteen-point-eight.

142
00:06:37,900 --> 00:06:40,178
That means a twenty-four-gigabyte card offers a

143
00:06:40,178 --> 00:06:42,907
realistic path to a moderately quantized

144
00:06:42,907 --> 00:06:45,323
twenty-seven-billion model while leaving some room

145
00:06:45,323 --> 00:06:46,520
for the rest of the runtime.

146
00:06:46,840 --> 00:06:49,252
But pay attention to the words, some room.

147
00:06:49,252 --> 00:06:51,716
The model's native context specification is not

148
00:06:51,716 --> 00:06:53,927
a promise that the full advertised window

149
00:06:53,927 --> 00:06:56,390
will fit in twenty-four gigabytes of VRAM.

150
00:06:56,710 --> 00:06:59,502
The Qwen model card describes a hybrid architecture

151
00:06:59,502 --> 00:07:02,231
rather than a stack of identical traditional attention

152
00:07:02,231 --> 00:07:05,680
layers. That affects how context memory grows, but

153
00:07:05,680 --> 00:07:07,860
it does not make the memory cost disappear.

154
00:07:08,180 --> 00:07:10,795
Image inputs and longer histories still affect the

155
00:07:10,795 --> 00:07:13,487
requirements, and the exact result depends on the

156
00:07:13,487 --> 00:07:16,576
implementation. Even at twenty-four gigabytes, there is

157
00:07:16,576 --> 00:07:19,620
a choice between quality, context, and speed.

158
00:07:19,940 --> 00:07:23,629
A less compressed model consumes more space, potentially

159
00:07:23,629 --> 00:07:26,609
leaving less capacity for a long conversation. A

160
00:07:26,609 --> 00:07:29,338
smaller quantization creates breathing room but may

161
00:07:29,338 --> 00:07:31,802
lose some precision. A longer context can

162
00:07:31,802 --> 00:07:34,061
also increase the time spent processing the

163
00:07:34,061 --> 00:07:36,040
prompt before you see the next answer.

164
00:07:36,360 --> 00:07:38,660
And twenty-four gigabytes on one card is not

165
00:07:38,660 --> 00:07:40,764
magically the same as twelve on each of

166
00:07:40,764 --> 00:07:44,454
two cards. Multi-GPU runtimes can split layers or

167
00:07:44,454 --> 00:07:46,701
tensors, but how that behaves depends on

168
00:07:46,701 --> 00:07:49,220
the runtime, the interconnect, and the workload.

169
00:07:49,540 --> 00:07:51,907
Adding the capacities is not the same as

170
00:07:51,907 --> 00:07:55,410
creating one physically unified pool of high-bandwidth memory.

171
00:07:55,735 --> 00:07:56,985
So what does a sensible user

172
00:07:56,985 --> 00:07:59,280
actually do? Not guess. Measure the

173
00:07:59,280 --> 00:08:01,050
configuration they intend to use.

174
00:08:01,414 --> 00:08:03,853
First, download a model version from a traceable

175
00:08:03,853 --> 00:08:07,628
publisher and note the exact quantization. Q-four-K-M and

176
00:08:07,628 --> 00:08:10,380
a one-bit-style format are not interchangeable just

177
00:08:10,380 --> 00:08:12,664
because both say the same model name.

178
00:08:12,664 --> 00:08:15,079
Record the file size, the runtime, and

179
00:08:15,079 --> 00:08:17,460
whether any vision component is being loaded.

180
00:08:17,782 --> 00:08:20,907
Second, pick a realistic starting context. For

181
00:08:20,907 --> 00:08:22,975
a first check on a smaller GPU,

182
00:08:22,975 --> 00:08:24,826
use a modest window and see whether

183
00:08:24,826 --> 00:08:26,650
it fits. You can grow it later.

184
00:08:26,970 --> 00:08:30,638
Ollama's current documentation is unusually explicit about why

185
00:08:30,638 --> 00:08:33,919
this matters: its default context allocation changes with

186
00:08:33,919 --> 00:08:36,371
the available VRAM, and it warns that

187
00:08:36,371 --> 00:08:39,148
larger context lengths require more memory. In

188
00:08:39,148 --> 00:08:41,552
Ollama, the process listing can also show

189
00:08:41,552 --> 00:08:43,463
whether a model is running entirely on

190
00:08:43,463 --> 00:08:45,660
the GPU or partly on the CPU.

191
00:08:45,993 --> 00:08:49,190
Third, check the memory estimate before you celebrate.

192
00:08:49,190 --> 00:08:52,291
LM Studio provides an estimate-only loading option that

193
00:08:52,291 --> 00:08:54,875
accounts for settings such as context length and

194
00:08:54,875 --> 00:08:58,288
GPU offload. Treat that as a useful estimate,

195
00:08:58,288 --> 00:09:00,524
not a substitute for running the model. Then

196
00:09:00,524 --> 00:09:02,620
watch actual usage during a real task.

197
00:09:02,960 --> 00:09:05,808
Fourth, test something more demanding than hello. Ask

198
00:09:05,808 --> 00:09:08,284
the model to explain an unfamiliar block of

199
00:09:08,284 --> 00:09:11,181
code and identify a specific bug. Give it

200
00:09:11,181 --> 00:09:13,597
a document and ask for three facts that

201
00:09:13,597 --> 00:09:15,592
are easy to verify. If you want a

202
00:09:15,592 --> 00:09:18,188
coding assistant, ask it to make a small,

203
00:09:18,188 --> 00:09:20,580
reviewable change and run the relevant test.

204
00:09:20,900 --> 00:09:23,041
Record whether it solved the task, how long

205
00:09:23,041 --> 00:09:25,421
it took, and whether it made mistakes.

206
00:09:25,421 --> 00:09:27,580
Speed without correctness is not a win.

207
00:09:27,929 --> 00:09:29,936
Fifth, try the same workload at a longer

208
00:09:29,936 --> 00:09:32,568
context. This is the step that exposes the

209
00:09:32,568 --> 00:09:35,056
difference between a model that merely loads

210
00:09:35,056 --> 00:09:36,871
and one you can actually work with.

211
00:09:36,871 --> 00:09:38,818
If it falls back to system RAM

212
00:09:38,818 --> 00:09:41,066
or slows dramatically, you have discovered the

213
00:09:41,066 --> 00:09:43,962
practical boundary of that configuration. You haven't

214
00:09:43,962 --> 00:09:46,840
discovered that the entire GPU is useless.

215
00:09:47,160 --> 00:09:50,068
One warning about published speed numbers. Tokens per

216
00:09:50,068 --> 00:09:52,328
second is not a universal property of a

217
00:09:52,328 --> 00:09:56,294
model. It depends on the GPU, quantization, backend,

218
00:09:56,294 --> 00:09:58,460
settings, and the type of work being measured.

219
00:09:58,782 --> 00:10:01,366
Prompt processing speed and the speed of generating

220
00:10:01,366 --> 00:10:03,698
new tokens are different things. A number

221
00:10:03,698 --> 00:10:05,609
from one card at a short context

222
00:10:05,609 --> 00:10:08,109
is not an honest head-to-head comparison with

223
00:10:08,109 --> 00:10:10,190
another card at a much longer context.

224
00:10:10,510 --> 00:10:13,495
And a final warning about parameter counts. Twenty-seven

225
00:10:13,495 --> 00:10:16,788
billion parameters describes the scale of a model.

226
00:10:16,788 --> 00:10:18,242
It does not tell you how well an

227
00:10:18,242 --> 00:10:20,875
ultra-compressed version will solve your task, whether it

228
00:10:20,875 --> 00:10:23,254
has the tools your application needs, or whether

229
00:10:23,254 --> 00:10:25,394
you can tolerate its latency. The useful

230
00:10:25,394 --> 00:10:27,353
model is not always the one with

231
00:10:27,353 --> 00:10:29,150
the largest number printed in its name.

232
00:10:29,470 --> 00:10:32,316
Here is the practical verdict. With eight gigabytes,

233
00:10:32,316 --> 00:10:35,008
start with a smaller, sensible four-bit model and

234
00:10:35,008 --> 00:10:39,203
short-to-moderate context. With twelve, a fourteen-billion four-bit model

235
00:10:39,203 --> 00:10:41,655
becomes a serious option, but keep a smaller

236
00:10:41,655 --> 00:10:44,000
model around for longer or lighter jobs.

237
00:10:44,320 --> 00:10:47,799
With sixteen, a heavily quantized twenty-seven-billion model

238
00:10:47,799 --> 00:10:49,854
is possible to experiment with, but a

239
00:10:49,854 --> 00:10:53,090
well-supported fourteen-billion setup may be more comfortable.

240
00:10:53,411 --> 00:10:56,644
With twenty-four, a four-bit twenty-seven-billion model becomes a

241
00:10:56,644 --> 00:11:00,214
much more realistic everyday candidate, provided your chosen

242
00:11:00,214 --> 00:11:03,210
context and runtime stay within the memory budget.

243
00:11:03,530 --> 00:11:05,514
None of those are promises that every card

244
00:11:05,514 --> 00:11:07,882
will achieve the same speed. They are starting

245
00:11:07,882 --> 00:11:10,923
points based on published model sizes and documented

246
00:11:10,923 --> 00:11:13,880
memory behavior. The only honest final verdict comes

247
00:11:13,880 --> 00:11:16,640
from testing the exact GPU and workload.

248
00:11:16,960 --> 00:11:19,960
Which brings us back to that six-gigabyte twenty-seven-billion

249
00:11:19,960 --> 00:11:22,844
model. The surprising part isn't that somebody compressed

250
00:11:22,844 --> 00:11:25,080
it that far. The surprising part is how

251
00:11:25,080 --> 00:11:27,448
often we treat download size as if it

252
00:11:27,448 --> 00:11:29,950
settles the entire question of local AI.

253
00:11:30,270 --> 00:11:32,737
It doesn't. Your real limit is the combination

254
00:11:32,737 --> 00:11:36,715
of weights, working memory, context, software support, and

255
00:11:36,715 --> 00:11:38,122
the quality you are willing to give

256
00:11:38,122 --> 00:11:40,573
up. Once you measure those things together,

257
00:11:40,573 --> 00:11:42,809
your GPU stops being a mystery and

258
00:11:42,809 --> 00:11:44,610
starts being a budget you can manage.

259
00:11:44,930 --> 00:11:46,940
If you're running local AI right now, tell

260
00:11:46,940 --> 00:11:49,139
me your graphics card, how much VRAM it

261
00:11:49,139 --> 00:11:51,615
has, the model you're using, and the longest

262
00:11:51,615 --> 00:11:55,257
context you've actually tested. Not the advertised maximum.

263
00:11:55,257 --> 00:11:57,456
The one that worked. That's the information that

264
00:11:57,456 --> 00:12:01,140
makes the next hardware decision useful for everybody.
